Q: How to debug dotnet core source code using Visual Studio Code A: <pre><code>{
  &quot;version&quot;: &quot;0.2.0&quot;,
  &quot;configurations&quot;: [
    {
      &quot;name&quot;: &quot;debugger name&quot;,
      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;coreclr&quot;,
      &quot;request&quot;: &quot;launch&quot;,
      &quot;program&quot;: &quot;${workspaceFolder}/path of the project dll&quot;,
      &quot;cwd&quot;: &quot;${workspaceFolder}&quot;,
      &quot;stopAtEntry&quot;: true,
      &quot;restart&quot;:true, //not recommended for the console program.
      &quot;console&quot;: &quot;integratedTerminal&quot;
    }
  ]
}
</code></pre>

Q: why is my program ending after 1st input? A: <p>This line creates an uninitialized object:</p>
<pre><code>struct NID my;
</code></pre>
<p>Proceeding to write to the uninitialized pointers in the structure is very wrong. These are wild pointers that almost certainly do not point to memory allocated to the program. These access violations crash your program.</p>
<p>By declaring these fields as <em>arrays</em> by guessing a maximum size (This example uses 16 but you can adjust them to your needs), proper memory is allocated when declaring the variable:</p>
<pre><code>struct DOB
{
        int date;
        char month[16];
        int year;
};
struct NID
{
        char name_bangla[16];
        char name_eng[16];
        char f_name[16];
        char m_name[16];
        struct DOB s;
        int ID;
};
</code></pre>
<p>Also make sure the <code>scanf</code> calls do not exceed the allocated memory by adding a limit to the format:</p>
<pre><code>scanf(&quot;%16s&quot;, my.name_bangla);
scanf(&quot;%16s&quot;, my.name_eng);
scanf(&quot;%16s&quot;, my.f_name);
// Etc
</code></pre>
